   "text": "This reads as 16\u2011O\u2011demethylrapamycin (sirolimus with a free OH in place of one methoxy), and I wrote the SMILES by adapting the known rapamycin SMILES to what the drawing shows. I did not re-derive each stereocentre from the wedges and hashes; I kept rapamycin's.\n\nReading the macrocycle from the pyran ring:\n- **Pyran ring:** an O\u2011bridged ring carries a methyl on a wedge, and the hemiketal carbon bears an OH.\n- **Amide/ester section:** the hemiketal carbon is followed by two adjacent C=O groups, then the piperidine N. The piperidine carbon next to N bears a hashed H and is bonded to a C(=O)\u2013O ester.\n- **Ester side:** the ester O leads to a CH that carries the side chain and the ring CH2 leading to a ketone.\n- **Side chain:** CH(CH3)\u2013CH2 leads to a cyclohexane ring bearing OMe and OH.\n- **Ketone to alkene:** the ketone is followed by CH(CH3) and a trisubstituted C=C(CH3).\n- **Hydroxyl and methoxy carbons:** next come CH(OH) and CH(OMe), then a C=O.\n- **Methylated section:** this C=O is followed by CH(CH3), CH2 and CH(CH3).\n- **Triene:** CH(CH3) leads into a conjugated triene, whose last carbon is the C(CH3)= that meets the next CH(OH).\n- **Closure:** CH(OH) then CH2 closes onto the pyran carbon.\n\nThe carbon next to the pyran CH2 carries a free OH, not the OMe that rapamycin has at that position. So I changed the terminal `OC` of the rapamycin SMILES to `O`.\n\nSMILES: C[C@@H]1CC[C@H]2C[C@@H](/C(=C/C=C/C=C/[C@H](C[C@H](C(=O)[C@@H]([C@@H](/C(=C/[C@H](C(=O)C[C@H](OC(=O)[C@@H]3CCCCN3C(=O)C(=O)[C@@]1(O2)O)[C@H](C)C[C@@H]4CC[C@H]([C@@H](C4)OC)O)C)/C)O)OC)C)C)/C)O"
  }
